{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"mudbrick-halfcrazed-pussycat","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context / Problem\n\n**This is a DIAGNOSABILITY fix, not a correctness one. The behaviour is already safe.**\nRead this first so nobody later mistakes it for another fail-open: bug\n`505d-b2c5-734f-47d9` made the claim gate fail CLOSED on a degraded basis, so whichever\nway the resolution fails the gate now refuses to certify. Nothing here changes a verdict.\nWhat is left is that an operator reading the box's logs cannot tell WHICH failure happened.\n\n`gate_ref_hash_basis()` (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/manifest.py`, added by 505d) wraps the\nwhole resolution in one broad `except Exception` and emits a single message:\n\n    current gate-ref snapshot unavailable; hashing the working tree\n\nTwo materially different faults collapse into that one line:\n\n1. **The ref does not resolve** — `resolve_ref(...)` raises `SnapshotRefError`. Benign and\n   expected in an ephemeral clone: the review-bot's `clone_change_ref` does `git init` +\n   `git fetch <url> <patchset-ref>` and configures no `origin` remote at all, so this fires\n   on 100% of review-bot runs. It is NOT actionable.\n2. **The ref resolved but the snapshot could not be materialized** — `_cache.acquire(...)`\n   raises. On the box's blobless checkout this is the interesting case:\n   `repo_snapshot._ensure_blobs_present` is documented \"Deliberately BEST-EFFORT and silent\n   on failure\", so a failed blob top-up plus a failed lazy fetch surfaces here. Disk\n   pressure, a corrupt or evicted cache entry, and a lock timeout land here too. This IS\n   actionable.\n\nCase 1 is routine noise; case 2 means the box cannot read the tree its gates are supposed\nto verify against. Today they are indistinguishable without attaching a debugger, so triage\nhas to guess — and because case 1 is so loud, case 2 is easy to dismiss as more of the same.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nThe log distinguishes the two, and the distinction is available to a caller that wants to\nreport it. An unresolvable ref stays a low-noise, expected outcome; a materialization\nfailure is identifiable as a distinct, operator-actionable fault.\n\n## Approach\n\nKeep it small. Inside `gate_ref_hash_basis` only:\n\n1. Split the single `try` so `resolve_ref` and `_cache.acquire` are caught separately, and\n   emit a distinct message for each (naming the ref in both cases; naming the resolved SHA\n   in the materialization case).\n2. Narrow the catches to what those calls actually raise (`SnapshotRefError` /\n   `SnapshotFetchError` for the ref arm), keeping a broad backstop so the function retains\n   its never-raises contract — that contract is load-bearing and must not regress.\n3. Optionally record which of the two occurred on `GateRefBasis` (e.g. a `cause` field), so\n   the claim gate's denial reason can say \"could not be resolved\" vs \"could not be\n   materialized\". Only if it stays cheap; the log split is the required part.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Any change to WHETHER the basis degrades, or to the fail-closed guard 505d added.\n- Any change to `_hash_basis`'s signature, contract, or working-tree fallback (ADR 0002; the\n  operator-approved decision on `72d9-8fbd-3a9e-48a6`).\n- Fixing the underlying materialization failures themselves.\n- Any new verdict literal. `bugfix_size_gate.KNOWN_VERDICTS` stays untouched.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An unresolvable gate ref and a failed snapshot materialization emit DISTINCT log\n      messages, each naming the ref (and the materialization case naming the resolved SHA).\n- [ ] `gate_ref_hash_basis` still never raises, and still returns the working-tree path with\n      `degraded=True` for BOTH faults — the 505d fail-closed behaviour is unchanged.\n- [ ] `source=local` is still not reported as degraded.\n- [ ] A test drives both arms separately and asserts the two are distinguishable (the\n      materialization arm by making `_cache.acquire` fail with the ref resolvable, so it is\n      not merely the ref arm again).\n- [ ] `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_ref_coherence.py` stays green unchanged, including\n      `test_attested_claim_gate_refuses_to_certify_when_the_gate_ref_is_unresolvable`.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the default pytest suite exit zero.\n","id":"6845-923d-ce78-44e9","parent_id":"","priority":3,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Distinguish an unresolvable gate ref from a failed snapshot materialization in gate_ref_hash_basis"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787964747709426785,"uuid":"169db0c4-01e3-4350-b035-729c6da89d45"}